## 3. How They All Connect With Each Other (The Wiring)

Here is a visual map showing exactly how data travels between each component:

```
[ Your Phone (Expo App) ]
         │
         │ (1) Sends audio over public HTTPS (Internet)
         ▼
[ Cloud Run: core-api ] ──(2) Private Unix Socket──► [ Cloud SQL Database ]
         │                                              (Stores session row)
         │ (3) Streams raw audio internally
         ▼
 ┌────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │ Which instrument is being played?      │
 ├───────────────────┬────────────────────┤
 │ Piano / Guitar?   │ Voice / Violin?    │
 │ (Chords)          │ (Solo Line)        │
 └─────────┬─────────┴──────────┬─────────┘
           │                    │
           ▼                    ▼
[ Cloud Run: worker-poly ]   [ Cloud Run: worker-mono ]
(Spotify Basic Pitch AI)     (CREPE Pitch AI)
           │                    │
           └──────────┬─────────┘
                      │ (4) Returns calculated notes, cents error & timing
                      ▼
[ Cloud Run: core-api ]
         │
         │ (5) Saves scores & note events
         ▼
[ Cloud SQL Database ]
         │
         │ (6) Returns full result JSON to phone
         ▼
[ Your Phone (Expo App) ] ──► Draws Green/Amber/Red Piano Roll & Timing bars!
```

### The 3 Key Connection Secrets:
1. **The Mobile App only ever talks to `core-api`**:
   The phone never connects directly to the database or to the heavy AI workers. This protects your database password and keeps the phone app super fast and simple.
2. **`core-api` talks to Cloud SQL via a Private Unix Socket**:
   The database port is **not** open to the public internet. It communicates through a secure internal tunnel (`/cloudsql/practice-tracker-2026-10:asia-south1:practice-tracker-db`), making it immune to outside hackers.
3. **All Cloud Services live in the same house (`asia-south1` Mumbai)**:
   Because Cloud Run, Cloud SQL, and Artifact Registry are all located in Google's Mumbai data center, talking between them takes less than 5 milliseconds!

